    <summary>Network scanner for field techs. No install required, no telemetry.</summary>
    <description>Fast local network scanner for Windows, built for field techs. One click maps the subnet - IP, hostname, MAC, vendor, device type, and open ports - with no admin console and no subscription.

- ARP cache + parallel ping sweep discovery; a second ARP pass catches phones and devices that block ICMP
- TCP port scan across 30+ common service ports with active fingerprinting: HTTP title, SSH banner, TLS cert, NetBIOS, SNMP, ICMP TTL
- mDNS (Bonjour) and SSDP (UPnP) discovery spots Chromecasts, printers, Sonos, AirPlay, Roku, Plex, and Synology devices
- MAC vendor lookup across the full IEEE registries (MA-L, MA-M, MA-S) with randomized-MAC detection; vendor database refreshable in-app
- Weighted-score classifier identifies routers, hypervisors, Windows and Linux boxes, printers, NAS, cameras, IoT, mobile, and more
- Right-click to copy IP/MAC/hostname, launch RDP/SSH/browser, or override a device type; SSH asks which account to use and remembers it per device
- Permanent Devices, Services, Topology, Keep Alive and Terminal views, each keeping its own state
- Topology (F8) infers how the network fits together, with movable multi-select device boxes, four arrangement modes, and full-resolution image or SVG export
- Keep Alive (F9) watches any number of devices at once as status cards with latency sparklines, packet loss, per-device checks and event logs
- Embedded terminals run PowerShell 7, ping and SSH with KillerShell's prompt and the KillerScripts module carried inside the EXE
- A speed test (F4) runs in the terminal, using the Ookla CLI when installed and a built-in HTTP test when it is not
- Deep Scan rescans every discovered host exhaustively; scan profiles (Ctrl+Shift+P) remember targets and can follow with Deep Scan
- Scan history (Ctrl+H) compares runs for added, missing and changed devices, or shows a saved run in full, with trusted-device baselines and unknown-device alerts
- A service-centric view (F7) organizes results by service, port and device
- Export follows the view: devices as CSV or HTML, services as CSV, topology as PNG, JPG or SVG, the Keep Alive run as CSV or a page, and the terminal session as text
- Headless command line for scripts and RMM: /scan, /probe, /network, offline /vendor, /export to CSV, JSON or HTML, filters, sorting, limits, timeouts, and real exit codes
- Scan several networks in one pass: comma-separated CIDR blocks, single hosts, and ranges
- Thirteen themes, localized in fifteen languages
- Keyboard shortcuts on F1, with a shortcut list and a color-coded keyboard map, plus an app-wide size control from 70% to 250%
- One self-contained Windows EXE, no runtime install; installs per-user or for all users, or runs portable
- GPLv3, local-only, no account, no telemetry
    </description>
    <releaseNotes>https://github.com/SteveTheKiller/KillerScan/releases/tag/vREPLACE_VERSION</releaseNotes>
